  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA surface low pressure system over the Great Lakes is forecasted\nby latest high resolution forecast model guidances to move \ndirectly over eastern New York and western New England tomorrow\nafternoon. As the weather system approaches, scattered rain\nshowers begin from west to east tomorrow morning and continue\nthroughout the day tomorrow as the center of the low arrives. \nThe nature of rain shower activity is forecasted to be light to\nmoderate (a steady normal rainfall) and with the recent dry \nstretch, we are expecting no flooding impacts. Rainfall totals\ncan range from 0.1 to 0.5 inches. This beneficial rainfall does\ncome to an end late Saturday night into Sunday morning from low\nelevations (such as river valleys) to high elevations (such as \nthe eastern Catskills, southern Vermont, Berkshires, and \nsouthern Adirondacks) due to lingering moisture to help produce\nadditional chances of rain showers in the higher terrain\nlocations Sunday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nEnsemble forecast model guidances are in good agreement for\ndaily low to medium chances of precipitation from Tuesday into \nthe middle of next week across eastern New York and western New \nEngland. While one scenario continues to support low chances of \nprecipitation in valley locations, depending on how the track of\nthe weather systems move through next week the valleys could \nsee drier conditions versus higher terrain locations. \nTemperatures in the morning are trending to be below 35 degrees \nacross eastern New York and western New England from Wednesday \nonwards as a colder air mass above the surface arrives. These \nunsettled weather conditions could support for the highest \nterrain of the southern Adirondacks on grassy surfaces with less\nthan 0.1 inches of snow for the middle to end of next week. \nWhile probabilities are currently low (less than 15 percent) for\nsnowfall to accumulate across the southern Adirondacks, the \ntrends are supporting for cooler temperatures with lows in the \nteens to 20s and highs in the 30s for the southern Adirondacks.\nMeanwhile, everywhere else across eastern New York and western\nNew England low temperatures next week range in the 20s with\nhighs in the 40s and 50s, a much cooler temperature trend than\nwe have seen in the preceding week.",
